# Caddyfile.example — TLS termination for munnel with on-demand per-subdomain certs.
#
# Rename to `Caddyfile` and set MUNNEL_DOMAIN in your environment (docker
# compose passes it through). Each *.tunnels.example.com subdomain gets its
# own Let's Encrypt certificate on first request via on-demand TLS.
#
# Caddy v2.8+ requires a permission module for on-demand TLS to prevent abuse
# (a stranger could otherwise force your server to issue certs for arbitrary
# domains). The `ask` endpoint below is munnel-server's --ask-addr listener
# (internal to the compose network): it approves only your apex and
# subdomains with a live tunnel, refusing everything else, so nobody can make
# the server request certificates for made-up names. No DNS-provider plugin
# or wildcard certificate is needed: each subdomain's A record (the wildcard)
# resolves to this host, so the HTTP-01 challenge succeeds.
#
# If you prefer a wildcard certificate instead, build Caddy with a DNS
# provider plugin (e.g. cloudflare), drop the `*.{$MUNNEL_DOMAIN}` block's
# `tls { on_demand }`, and use `tls { dns cloudflare {env.CF_API_TOKEN} }`.

{
	# No ACME contact on purpose: Let's Encrypt refuses an example.com address
	# (invalidContact) and no longer sends expiry mail. With no email Caddy has
	# no ZeroSSL fallback either, so every certificate comes from Let's Encrypt.
	on_demand_tls {
		ask http://munnel-server:8081/check
	}
}

{$MUNNEL_DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy munnel-server:8080
}

*.{$MUNNEL_DOMAIN} {
	tls {
		on_demand
	}
	reverse_proxy munnel-server:8080
}